@startuml ModbusBusOwnership
!include theme.puml

' File-local: stock PlantUML gives storage a light fill, which leaves the
' theme's white text unreadable. Same fix the other diagrams use.
skinparam storage {
    BackgroundColor #4682B4
    BorderColor #ffffff
    FontColor #ffffff
}

title RS485 Bus Ownership - Release and Reacquire\nOne bus, three devices: releasing it means nothing can actuate

package "The physical constraint" {
    component [ModbusCoordinator\n500 ms ticks, 10-tick / 5 s cycle\nschedules every device on the SAME bus] as Coordinator

    storage "MB8ART\ntemperatures" as MB8ART
    storage "ANDRTF3\nroom sensor" as ANDRTF3
    storage "RYN4\nrelays - burner and pumps" as RYN4

    storage "RYN4 DELAY watchdog\n10 s per energised relay\nrenewed on every RYN4_SET tick" as Watchdog

    Coordinator --> MB8ART : reads
    Coordinator --> ANDRTF3 : reads
    Coordinator --> RYN4 : RYN4_SET\n+ DELAY renewal
    Watchdog --> RYN4 : de-energises EVERYTHING\nwithin 10 s, unaided
}

note bottom of Coordinator
  All three share one RS485 bus, so the obvious design -
  "stop polling and let the safety layer stop the burner" -
  is impossible: once the bus is released, nothing can
  actuate anything. The release has to be refused up front
  instead, and the hardware has to be able to finish the job
  without us.
end note

package "Ownership lifecycle" {
    storage "Bus HELD (normal)\nModbusCoordinator owns the bus" as Held
    storage "Bus RELEASED\nanother master owns it" as Released
}

Held --> Released : bus_release [seconds]\nonly if mayRelease() == NONE
Released --> Held : bus_acquire\nor on-device expiry

note right of Held
  Entry REFUSES, it does not orchestrate.
  BusReleasePolicy::mayRelease() returns a reason,
  checked in this priority order:

  1. BURNER_ACTIVE      a firing relay is closed
  2. DEMAND_PRESENT     demand is armed - it precedes
                        the relays by a pre-purge
  3. PUMP_ACTIVE        any pump relay is closed
  4. BURNER_SEQUENCING  SM not in IDLE / LOCKOUT / ERROR
  5. EMERGENCY_LATCHED  emergency stop latched

  PUMP_ACTIVE is there because releasing during
  post-purge, pump overrun or emergency dissipation
  would trap residual heat with no flow.
end note

note right of Released
  While released:
  - the burner CANNOT be armed -
    BurnerStateMachine::setHeatDemand consults the policy
  - firing bits are forced out of the DESIRED mask BEFORE
    bus access resumes
  - the sensor fallback does not escalate: the only thing
    that re-evaluated it was driven BY MB8ART reads, and
    "stale because we stopped asking" is not the same
    fault as "stale and we don't know why" (9bf6be5)

  The window expires ON-DEVICE: with the bus released there
  is no guaranteed path for a remote acquire, so a dropped
  network must not park the controller.
  Default 600 s, min 30 s, max 900 s hard cap.
  Extending only ever lengthens, never shortens.

  A reboot mid-window is safe by construction: nothing is
  persisted, so the controller comes back with a busy bus
  and the other master's tool fails visibly.
end note

Released --> Watchdog : ticks withheld

note bottom of Watchdog
  This is what makes the whole feature safe, and it was NOT
  designed for it. Every relay switched ON is held by an RYN4
  DELAY watchdog, renewed on each RYN4_SET tick. Withhold the
  ticks and the board drops burner, pumps and all by itself -
  no cooperation from the firmware required.

  So a released bus converges on the same physical state as
  pulling the controller's power.
end note

package "Observability" {
    component "boiler/status/bus\nretained, published on change\nreleased + seconds left, or the end reason\n(timeout or command)" as BusStatus
    component "boiler/cmd/system\npayload: bus_release <seconds>\npayload: bus_acquire" as BusCmd
}

BusCmd --> Held : bus_release
Released --> BusStatus : publishes

legend right
  Source of truth:
  include/modules/control/BusReleasePolicy.h (read the
  header comment - it explains the why)
  src/core/ModbusBusRelease.cpp
  src/core/ModbusCoordinator.cpp

  Shipped in a4fc936. First real use 2026-10-25
  14:49:40-14:52:59 CET: 199 s of a 900 s window for a
  room-sensor firmware update. Nothing latched.
endlegend

@enduml
